The two liberties of the connected C1-C2 black stones are exactly B1 and B2. Either white first move at one of those points allows black to answer immediately at the remaining point and capture the isolated white stone (standard capture rule: the placement fills the last liberty of the single-stone white group, removal occurs before the capturer checks its own liberties, and the capture re-opens a liberty at the captured point).  

No other intersection produces a forcing sequence that removes either black cluster regardless of black's replies; the B3-B4 stones retain connection and external liberties (A3, A4, etc.) that white cannot close in one move. Thus the only correct list of killing first moves is the empty set.

